Life as a PhD student.

I think it is really easy to want to do a PhD, but to not actually know what that means for how your day-to-day life is going to look.
I think because so much of the discussion online is about the intensity of PhDs (especially because the US system often combines them with master’s degrees and the Internet is very American-centric), 
I had a perception going in that all I would have time to do during my work days was research. 
I would just read papers and write code and write papers. 
It turns out, my life has way more variety and way less intensity than that! 
I actually do so many things that aren’t reading and writing!

As well as this, the lab I work at recently had a careers day where we had many alumni come and give talks.
One thing that really stood out to me was that Dr Steph Yardley made a slide about some of the things she does day-to-day as a research fellow.
It made me realise that I'd never really thought about what my daily life could look like if I choose to pursue an academic career after my PhD.
With how useful that information was to me, I thought I would write an equivalent piece on what day-to-day life looks like as a PhD student.
This is something that changes constantly as you progress through your PhD, so I'm going to talk in this about what my life looked like six months in, and what it looks like now I'm one year in.
I hope to write another one of these when I come to the end of my second year and so on, to capture how day-to-day life has changed throughout my PhD.

This is coming from a PhD student in solar physics based in England.

6 months into my PhD, my week typically contained
* Emails and admin
* Journal club
* Presentation writing for a group meeting
* Reading papers
* Public engagement training
* Data analysis
* Supervisor meetings
* Research group meetings
* PhD student social club
* SunPy meetings

Reading papers was quite a dominant feature of my life for the first six months of my PhD.
There is normally a huge amount to learn when you first start, and my department also checked our progress via a literature review six months into our PhD.
This meant I was doing a lot of reading papers!

A year into my PhD, my week typically contained
* Emails and admin
* Writing conference talks
* Reading papers
* Data analysis
* Supervisor meetings
* PhD student social club
* Paper writing
* Instrument team meetings
* Public engagement preparation

The big obvious change between six months and one year is that I am now writing a paper!
To add
- Journal club and SunPy meeting attendance have declined due to workload elsewhere
- Talks have transitioned from group to conference talks
- No conferences in first six months
- More international travel after first six months
- Data analysis has changed from learning how to do things to getting results
- Starting to build a network hence instrument team meetings
- Public engagement is not a universal experience
- Have moved towards delivering public engagement rather than training to do so
